  "content": {
    "problemStatement": "For a string SSS, we define f(S)f(S)f(S) as the number of **distinct palindromic†^{\\dagger}† substrings‡^{\\ddagger}‡** of SSS.\n\nFor example, f(abaa)=4f(abaa) = 4f(abaa)=4 because we can find the palindrome substrings a,b,aaa, b, aaa,b,aa and abaabaaba.\n\nCall a string SSS good if f(S)≤5f(S) \\le 5f(S)≤5, i.e. it has at most 555 distinct palindrome substrings.\n\nYou are given an integer NNN. Construct any good string of length NNN using only lowercase Latin characters. It can be proven that at least one good string exists. You are allowed to print any good string.\n\n†^{\\dagger}† A string is called palindromic if it reads the same forwards and backwards.\n\n‡^{\\ddagger}‡ A substring is a string that can be obtained by deleting some (possibly zero) characters from the beginning and some (possibly zero) characters from the end.\n\n\nInput Format\n\nThe first line of input will contain a single integer TTT, denoting the number of test cases.\nThe first and only line of input contains a single integer NNN.\n\n\nOutput Format\nFor each test case, output a string SSS of length NNN, using only lowercase Latin characters, with f(S)≤5f(S) \\le 5f(S)≤5.",
    "constraints": [
      "1≤T≤1001 \\le T \\le 1001≤T≤100",
      "1≤N≤1001 \\le N \\le 1001≤N≤100"
    ],
    "inputFormat": "",
    "outputFormat": "",
    "notes": [],
